  <data name="AboutMeLogisticsText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>I have extensive practical experience in designing end-to-end business workflows for logistics and warehouse management automation in large holding companies. I developed comprehensive modules for inter-branch inventory movement based on two-stage logic: shipping goods at internal transfer prices while retaining stock on the holding's balance sheet, automatically generating transit receipt drafts, and their final recognition on the end-destination branch warehouses. To ensure process security, I implemented flexible Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) models, restricting the privileges of line staff based on their authorized locations.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AboutMeMainText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>A C# Developer with 12 years of extensive experience in enterprise development. I specialize in designing complex backend systems, building fault-tolerant integration solutions between the 1C ecosystem and external services, and deep performance optimization of relational databases. I possess a rare skill — detailed knowledge of the internal table structures and DBMS of 1C configurations (Trade Management, HR &amp; Payroll, Document Management), which allows me to calculate tables and build data selections directly via SQL queries, bypassing the heavy standard platform syntax.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="AboutMeMobileText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>In addition to backend development, I successfully design client-server mobile architectures using Flutter to automate field personnel operations (sales agents, transport logistics). I specialize in the Offline-first concept: I implemented guaranteed geodata delivery mechanisms where coordinates are buffered in the smartphone's local database when connection is lost, sent in batches to the server via SignalR / REST API once connection is restored, then validated with a successful delivery flag (send = true), and automatically cleared to prevent device storage overflow.</value>
  </data>
